"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][15]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M15-L1 – Research questions, literature searches and critical appraisal of sources<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5, M6, M10.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a testable research\nquestion, document a search strategy and assess the scope of evidence\nprovided by a source.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a practical problem to a research question<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional university workshop wants to estimate, on the evening\nbefore each opening day, how many material sets will be issued the\nfollowing day. A better forecast might reduce shortages and surpluses.\nHowever, the statement ‘AI improves planning’ cannot be tested: neither\nthe comparison method nor the target, time frame or population is\nspecified. The research question is therefore: does a predefined,\nreservation-based forecast reduce the mean absolute error of daily\ndemand compared with a constant forecast when both are learnt from the\nsame past opening days?<\/p>\n<p>The target is the number of sets issued per opening day, and the\ninformation available is limited to the previous evening. The feature is\na binary indicator of a high level of advance reservations. The\ninvestigation is limited to synthetic workshop data; an association\nbetween reservations and issues is not claimed to be causal. A\nforecasting study assesses predictions; an impact study would need to\ninvestigate an intervention and a suitable comparison design. Even if a\nmodel performs better, this does not establish an economic benefit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Targeted literature search<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001578_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Suchblöcke führen zur Suchstrategie und anschließend zur Prüfung, Dokumentation und Begrenzung der Aussage.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Suchblöcke führen zur Suchstrategie\nund anschließend zur Prüfung, Dokumentation und Begrenzung der\nAussage.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Von der Forschungsfrage zur Evidenz.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The question is divided into search blocks: subject (demand\nforecasting, daily counts), method (baseline, prediction) and evaluation\n(time split, absolute error). Synonyms are joined with OR, and different\nblocks with AND. One might start with a library catalogue and an\nacademic search engine, examine titles and abstracts and follow\nreferences. A search log records the date, database, exact search\nstring, filters, number of results, inclusion and exclusion criteria and\nreasons for selection. The number of results changes; it must not be\ninvented retrospectively. A full systematic review is not required here;\nsuch a review would additionally conduct selection and appraisal\naccording to a suitable protocol.<\/p>\n<p>For an article on forecasting retail sales, for example, check\nwhether the target is continuous or discrete, what temporal split was\nused and whether the baseline was determined fairly. A good result in\nanother market is a starting point, not proof for one’s own context.\nBackward searching examines cited references; forward searching\nidentifies later studies that cite the article. Both can reduce gaps in\nthe initial keyword search.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence matrix and critical appraisal of sources<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assessment dimension<\/th>\n<th>Question<\/th>\n<th>Implication for the project<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Subject relevance<\/td>\n<td>Same target and forecast horizon?<\/td>\n<td>Limit transferability<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Study design<\/td>\n<td>Future data excluded from training?<\/td>\n<td>Check leakage risk<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uncertainty<\/td>\n<td>Variation or intervals reported?<\/td>\n<td>Do not generalise a single result<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Provenance<\/td>\n<td>Authors, version and publication venue identifiable?<\/td>\n<td>Identify the source unambiguously<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>API documentation explains how a method is implemented; a research\narticle can establish its empirical properties under particular\nconditions. A blog can provide orientation but needs to be supplemented\nwith appropriate specialist sources for central claims. Peer-reviewed\npublications can also have methodological weaknesses. In the literature\nlog, distinguish source findings, one’s own interpretation and remaining\nquestions. Cite paraphrases; do not trace third-party illustrations or\nreproduce them without permission. All examples and diagrams for this\neducational project were created independently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Turn ‘Reservations help’ into a falsifiable hypothesis. Solution: on\nthe later test days, the MAE of the predefined group model is lower than\nthe MAE of the constant baseline. The formulation must allow for failure\nas well. Which source establishes that less material is actually wasted\nin one’s own workshop? None of the methodological sources; this would\nrequire a separate investigation of real outcomes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-09-389662\">DFG:\nGuideline 9 – Research design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – research design and state of\nresearch. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/project-design/pd-overview/pd-checklist/\">The\nTuring Way: Getting Started Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – project planning,\nobjectives, methodological and organisational prerequisites.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M15-L2 – Data collection plan, baseline and experimental protocol<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M7, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define the variables to be\ncollected and target measurement time, specify a fair reference and plan\nan experiment that can be checked in advance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Measurement plan and information cut-off<\/h2>\n<p>The workshop study uses exactly one row per opening day. In the\nteaching dataset, the time variable <code>tag<\/code> (day) is a\nconsecutive ordinal number, <code>hoch<\/code> (high) is set to 0 or 1 on\nthe previous evening, and <code>menge<\/code> (quantity) is the actual\nnumber issued at the end of the day. A real study would also need to\ndocument when reservations are counted, what counts as an issue and how\nreturns are handled. Closed days are outside the target population. An\nempty measurement field is not an observed zero. Data provenance and\npossible outages are recorded.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field<\/th>\n<th>Type<\/th>\n<th>Collection time<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>tag<\/td>\n<td>positive integer<\/td>\n<td>opening calendar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hoch<\/td>\n<td>0 or 1<\/td>\n<td>previous evening<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>menge<\/td>\n<td>non-negative integer<\/td>\n<td>end of day<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>A threshold selected from test data violates the temporal information\ncut-off. The exercise assumes the binary indicator is already supplied\nas an input. It does not investigate the selection of an optimal\nthreshold.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Baseline and test boundary<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001570_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Tage 1 bis 6 trainieren die beiden Verfahren; Tage 7 bis 12 bewerten unveränderte Modelle.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Tage 1 bis 6 trainieren die beiden\nVerfahren; Tage 7 bis 12 bewerten unveränderte Modelle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Zeitliche Trennung im Experiment.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A reference model should be simple and actually available. Here the\nconstant forecast is the arithmetic mean of the training quantities (not\nthe median, which is optimal for absolute error; both are 10 in the\nteaching case). The comparison model computes its own training mean for\neach feature group. If a group is absent from training, it uses the\noverall mean as a predefined fallback rule. The first six days form the\ntraining set, the following six the test set. These small sample sizes\nserve a teaching purpose; they are insufficient to demonstrate stable\npredictive performance for a real operation. A larger study would use\ntemporally separate validation between the training and test periods,\nfor example through rolling evaluation windows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Experimental protocol<\/h2>\n<p>The protocol is written and versioned before evaluation. In this\nconstructed exercise the rules are given; it is not an actually\nregistered study. The primary metric is mean absolute error in sets per\nday:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/munderover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{MAE}=\\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{i=1}^n |y_i-\\hat y_i|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The test rules remain the same for both methods: the same days, the\nsame target variable, no further fitting during the test period and no\nchanges based on its results. The improvement in sets/day is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=\\operatorname{MAE}_{B}-\\operatorname{MAE}_{G}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nPositive values favour the group model. Signed errors are additionally\nstored as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_i-\\hat y_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto detect systematic underprediction. A predefined minimum benefit in a\nreal project could be derived from material and shortage costs; an\narbitrary threshold is not presented here as economically justified.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Checkpoint<\/th>\n<th>Evidence<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Data collection<\/td>\n<td>field definitions, outage log<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Split<\/td>\n<td>training/test boundary in configuration<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methods<\/td>\n<td>fixed model rules and fallback rule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation<\/td>\n<td>MAE, individual errors, unchanged test set<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Changing the method after the test may be useful, but must be\ndocumented as exploratory and evaluated on new independent data. The\nseparation matters more than an impressive result.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A student computes the mean of all twelve days and uses it as the\nconstant baseline. What is wrong? Solution: she uses six future target\nvalues that were unavailable at the respective forecast times. The\nreference is therefore unfair. With training quantities 7, 9, 8, 11, 13,\n12, the mean permitted in advance is 60/6 = 10 sets.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-09-389662\">DFG:\nGuideline 9 – Research design<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – research design and state of\nresearch. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/project-design/pd-overview/pd-checklist/\">The\nTuring Way: Getting Started Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – project planning,\nobjectives, methodological and organisational prerequisites.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html?highlight=kfold\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices, §12.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – data leakage and\ndata separation. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.dummy.DummyClassifier.html\">scikit-learn:\nDummyClassifier<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – feature-independent reference models for\nclassification; analogy to the regression case.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M15-L3 – Building a reproducible analysis project<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M3, M7, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Separate data, code,\nconfiguration and results, and repeat a deterministic teaching run with\nvalidation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From individual files to a verifiable workflow<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001684_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Daten samt Hash, Konfiguration und Code führen über die validierte Analyse zu Ergebnis und Protokoll.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Daten samt Hash, Konfiguration und Code\nführen über die validierte Analyse zu Ergebnis und\nProtokoll.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Bausteine eines reproduzierbaren Laufs.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An analysis project is reproducible if its results can be recreated\nfrom described inputs and processing steps. In this context, this does\nnot mean that independent data collection produces the same values. In\nthe workshop project, raw data, scripts, configuration and derived\nresults are kept separate. A data dictionary describes types, units and\ntemporal references. A README describes the launch command, expected\noutput, required environment and limitations. Version control provides a\ntraceable record of which version produced the analysis. Data with\nrestricted access must not accidentally enter a public repository.<\/p>\n<p>A small directory structure might look like this (the original German\nfilenames are retained):<\/p>\n<pre class=\"text\"><code>werkstatt/\n  README.md\n  data/tage.csv\n  data/datenwoerterbuch.md\n  config.json\n  src/analyse.py\n  tests/\n  results/<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Raw data are not manually overwritten in a spreadsheet. Corrections\nare made as versioned transformations with reasons. A hash identifies\nthe exact bytes of a file; it does not by itself prove the accuracy or\nprovenance of its contents. Larger projects also document dependencies,\noperating systems, model versions and sources of randomness. A seed\nalone does not guarantee bitwise identical results across platforms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Minimal executable analysis workflow<\/h2>\n<p>The following complete example uses only the Python standard library.\nIt deliberately uses twelve constructed rows, not real workshop data.\nThe data are embedded as an ASCII CSV string so that the example runs\nwithout path configuration. In the project documentation, the same rows\ncan be stored in a separate CSV. The configuration contains the fixed\nboundary of six days. Outputs can be saved as JSON and compared with an\nexpected result. The German field names from L2 are retained.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> csv, io, hashlib, json<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> statistics <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> mean<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>raw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&quot;tag,hoch,menge<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">1,0,7<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">2,0,9<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">3,0,8<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">4,1,11<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">5,1,13<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">6,1,12<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">7,0,9<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">8,1,12<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">9,0,8<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">10,1,14<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">11,1,11<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">12,1,15<\/span><span class=\"ch\">\\n<\/span><span class=\"st\">&quot;<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">tuple<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>, r)) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(csv.reader(io.StringIO(raw)))[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>:]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> [r[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows] <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">list<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">13<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-6\"><a href=\"#cb2-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(g <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> y <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, g, y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rows)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-7\"><a href=\"#cb2-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>cut <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-8\"><a href=\"#cb2-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>train, test <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rows[:cut], rows[cut:]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-9\"><a href=\"#cb2-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>baseline <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mean(y <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, _, y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> train)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-10\"><a href=\"#cb2-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>group <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-11\"><a href=\"#cb2-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> g <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-12\"><a href=\"#cb2-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    vals <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [y <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, k, y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> train <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> k <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> g]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-13\"><a href=\"#cb2-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    group[g] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mean(vals) <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> vals <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> baseline<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-14\"><a href=\"#cb2-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>errors_b <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>baseline) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, _, y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> test]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-15\"><a href=\"#cb2-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>errors_g <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [<span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(y<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>group[g]) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, g, y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> test]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-16\"><a href=\"#cb2-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> baseline <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> group <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>: <span class=\"dv\">12<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-17\"><a href=\"#cb2-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(errors_b) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">15<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(errors_g) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">7<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-18\"><a href=\"#cb2-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>result <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&quot;sha256&quot;<\/span>: hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest(),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-19\"><a href=\"#cb2-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"st\">&quot;cut&quot;<\/span>: cut, <span class=\"st\">&quot;baseline_mae&quot;<\/span>: mean(errors_b),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-20\"><a href=\"#cb2-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"st\">&quot;group_mae&quot;<\/span>: mean(errors_g)}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-21\"><a href=\"#cb2-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(json.dumps(result, sort_keys<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"va\">True<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>This run estimates both models using only the first six rows. It\nexpects MAE values of 2.5 and approximately 1.167. The assertions are\nregression tests for this fixed teaching file; with new data, different\nvalues may be correct. More general tests would also cover unseen\ngroups, missing values, incorrect time order and duplicate day\nidentifiers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reproduction and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A second person receives exactly the same input bytes, configuration\nand analysis code, runs the analysis in a documented Python 3\nenvironment and compares the hash and result. Different hashes initially\nindicate different inputs. If the hash and configuration are the same\nbut the result differs, the code version, libraries and numerical\nsettings are examples of things to investigate. For floating-point\nnumbers, a technically justified tolerance comparison is often more\nappropriate than exact equality of decimal text. In the small example,\nthe calculation is independent of random selection and external\nAPIs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>On paper, add a test for a duplicate day identifier. Solution: the\nnumber of unique identifiers must equal the number of rows, for example\n<code>len({r[0] for r in rows}) == len(rows)<\/code>. Why is a hash of\nthe result file alone insufficient? It reveals that a file has changed\nbut explains neither the inputs nor the analysis steps. A reproducible\nrun needs the entire mapping.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-12-389682\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – traceable documentation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/rdm/\">The\nTuring Way: Research Data Management<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – data management and reuse.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/renv/\">The\nTuring Way: Reproducible Environments<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – recording the computing\nenvironment. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M15-L4 – Evaluation and clear presentation of results<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M5, M6, M10, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate paired forecast\nerrors, describe uncertainty and limitations, and design a transparent\npresentation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparison on the same test days<\/h2>\n<p>Both methods were fitted on the first six days. The constant baseline\npredicts 10 sets, whereas the group model predicts 8 when advance\nreservations are low and 12 when they are high. The six later days are\nnow used exclusively for evaluation. The actual values were constructed\nfor the teaching example.<\/p>\n<table class=\"lc-table-width-99\">\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-16\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Day<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Actual<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Baseline<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Group model<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Absolute error B<\/th>\n<th class=\"text-right\">Absolute error G<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">7<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">9<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">8<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">0<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">14<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">4<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">2<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">11<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">15<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">10<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">12<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">5<\/td>\n<td class=\"text-right\">3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>15<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>G<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>7<\/mn><mn>6<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.167<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{MAE}_B=\\frac{15}{6}=2.5,\\qquad \\operatorname{MAE}_G=\\frac{7}{6}\\approx1.167.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta=8/6\\approx1.333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsets per day. The relative decrease is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">MAE<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>15<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>53.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta/\\operatorname{MAE}_B=8/15\\approx53.3\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(meaningful only for a positive baseline MAE). This is not equivalent to\na 53.3% cost saving. Both models were tested on exactly the same days;\nthe error differences are therefore paired, not two independent\nsamples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What the numbers can tell us<\/h2>\n<p>The daily differences in absolute errors are 0, 2, 2, 2, 0, 2. The\ngroup model is better on four days and equally good on two. Six\nartificially selected days do not establish a statistically supported\ngeneralisation to a workshop. In real observations, adjacent days can be\ndependent, for example because of the start of term or weekly patterns.\nAn ordinary bootstrap of independent rows or a t-interval based on six\ndependent daily values would then be unreliable. A later, sufficiently\nlarge test window with a documented temporal sampling plan is required;\nblock methods may account for dependence under suitable assumptions.\nSpurious precision in p-values from a constructed teaching dataset\nshould be avoided.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From a table to a results chart<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001583_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Gruppierte Balken für die absoluten Tagesfehler beider Modelle; Baseline 1, 2, 2, 4, 1, 5; Gruppenmodell 1, 0, 0, 2, 1, 3.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Gruppierte Balken für die absoluten\nTagesfehler beider Modelle; Baseline 1, 2, 2, 4, 1, 5; Gruppenmodell 1,\n0, 0, 2, 1, 3.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Prognosefehler im direkten Vergleich.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The diagram shows the two absolute errors for each day on a common\naxis starting at zero. Grouping preserves the paired structure. Another\npossible chart would show actual and forecast values over time;\nsystematic underprediction would be particularly visible there. For the\ngroup model, the mean signed error is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1+0+0+2-1+3)/6=5/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsets; positive values here indicate underprediction on average. For the\nbaseline it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">( -1+2-2+4+1+5)/6=9/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsets.<\/p>\n<p>In the text, identify the data, metric, model comparison and\nlimitations. The factual statement is: on six constructed test days, the\nMAE of the specified group model was lower than that of the reference.\nWhether a real workshop would benefit remains open. Every results chart\nmust make clear which measurements, units and samples it shows. A\ntruncated bar-chart axis would visually distort the differences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>On day 11, the error of both models is 1. Are their forecasts\ntherefore identical? Solution: no, the baseline predicts 10 and the\ngroup model 12 when the actual value is 11. The absolute errors are\nequal, but their signs are opposite. If shortages are expensive and\nsurpluses cheap, an asymmetric loss measure may be more appropriate; it\nmust be specified before the test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-12-389682\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – traceable documentation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/common_pitfalls.html?highlight=kfold\">scikit-learn:\nCommon pitfalls and recommended practices, §12.2<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – data leakage and\ndata separation. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M15-L5 – Data protection, consent and research ethics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M7, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>,\n<a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish data protection and\nethical reviews, and justify a design based on data minimisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What data are actually necessary?<\/h2>\n<p>The synthetic daily counts in this teaching study contain no\ninformation about real people. In a real workshop, reservation systems\ncould contain names, timestamps, identification numbers and specific\nmaterial lists. Even without names, a combination of attributes can make\nindividuals identifiable. The first question is therefore whether the\nresearch task needs individual reservations at all. For daily quantity\nforecasts, the proposed case requires only a sufficiently aggregated\ncount or indicator that has already been produced lawfully. Producing\naggregates from personal data is itself processing and requires a lawful\nframework.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Legal basis, information and consent<\/h2>\n<p>The GDPR applies to the processing of personal data within its scope.\nBefore collection, the purposes, necessary fields, responsibilities and\na valid legal basis must be determined. Consent is one possible basis\nunder Article 6, not the only one. Where relied upon, it must, among\nother requirements, be freely given, informed, specific and unambiguous;\nwithdrawal must be possible. In relationships of dependency, the\nvoluntary nature of consent requires particular scrutiny. Information\nunder Article 13 or 14 depends in part on whether data are obtained from\nthe data subject or another source. Special categories of data\nadditionally require an exception under Article 9. Research alone does\nnot provide blanket permission; the relevant legal requirements and\nsafeguards under Article 89 must be examined.<\/p>\n<p>Pseudonymisation replaces direct identifiers with codes and keeps the\nadditional information separately protected. Data that can be\nre-identified remain personal data. Genuine anonymisation requires an\nassessment of realistic possibilities for identification. A hash of an\neasily guessed identification number is insufficient. Data minimisation,\nlimited retention periods, access permissions and security must be built\ninto the design from the start. The specific legal position and local\nreview requirements must be clarified with the university and, where\nappropriate, data protection officers and relevant ethics bodies. This\nlesson does not provide legal advice for individual cases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safeguards and ethical assessment<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001545_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Einzelreservierungen nur nach Prüfung; bei offenem Risiko anhalten, sonst Aggregation, Tagesanalyse und Freigabeprüfung.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Einzelreservierungen nur nach Prüfung;\nbei offenem Risiko anhalten, sonst Aggregation, Tagesanalyse und\nFreigabeprüfung.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Datensparsame Forschung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The original workflow diagram separates the necessary data access\nfrom the analysis dataset. Before a real project begins, the workshop\nchecks whether aggregation is permissible; only the required daily\nfeatures are then transferred into an access-restricted analysis\ndataset. No identifier enters the model. The review before release of\nresults checks again whether rare combinations allow individuals to be\ninferred. If the purpose, legal basis or risk has not been sufficiently\nclarified, data are not collected or results are not published, as\napplicable.<\/p>\n<p>Ethics is more than formal legality: a forecast error may cause\nshortages that affect certain groups more often. A research plan\ndocuments potential harms, evaluates alternatives and identifies those\nresponsible for decisions. The total quantity investigated here does not\njustify individual assessment, ranking or access decisions. Data not\nneeded for forecasting are not collected as a precaution for other\npurposes. Open research does not mean making all raw data freely\navailable; where appropriate, code, synthetic examples and aggregated\nresults can be shared without disclosing protected information.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A project group wants to store the names and degree programmes of\neveryone making a reservation in order to forecast the total number of\nmaterial sets. What is an obvious alternative? Solution: a sufficiently\naggregated daily count or the predefined indicator, provided it is\nproduced lawfully and is suitable for the research question. Consent\nalone does not make unnecessary fields necessary. If the group poses a\nnew research question involving personal data, a new legal and ethical\nreview would be needed before collection.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/DE/ALL/?tid=111682012&amp;uri=celex%3A32016R0679\">Regulation\n(EU) 2016/679 – GDPR, Arts. 4–7, 9, 13–14, 25, 32, 89<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – legal\nframework; alternative EUR-Lex view opened; no case-specific review.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-10-389674\">DFG:\nGuideline 10 – Legal and ethical frameworks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – legal and ethical\nresponsibility. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/reproducible-research/rdm/\">The\nTuring Way: Research Data Management<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – data management and reuse.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M15-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M15-L6 – Project report, presentation and planning a Bachelor’s thesis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M15 – Bachelor: Academic research and Bachelor’s project (6 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/practice<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L1<\/a>\nto <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M15&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M15-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Plan a clearly scoped project,\nstructure a research report and present results with verifiable\nevidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The report as a verifiable argument<\/h2>\n<p>A good project report explains not only what was programmed, but why\nthe chosen steps address the research question. The workshop example\nprovides a small template. The introduction describes the need and\nquestion, the literature review justifies the comparison, and the\nmethods section explains data provenance, time boundary, models and\nmetric. The results section shows the six paired errors without making a\nnew model selection. The discussion considers validity, uncertainty and\nalternatives. The conclusion answers the question within the scope of\nthe data actually investigated. A reference list and appendices make\nevidence, data dictionary, code and run log easy to find.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-50\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Section<\/th>\n<th>Specific content in the teaching project<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Introduction<\/td>\n<td>daily demand, fixed forecast horizon<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Literature review<\/td>\n<td>models, comparison principle, limitations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Methods<\/td>\n<td>synthetic data, sequence, fixed rules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Results<\/td>\n<td>MAE 2.5 and 7/6 sets/day, individual errors<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discussion<\/td>\n<td>no real sample; no evidence of cost savings or causality<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>Results that contradict the hypothesis are also presented. Departures\nfrom the original plan must be documented with dates and reasons.\nSources are identified consistently and others’ contributions\nacknowledged; this also applies to data and software. If AI tools are\nused, local university rules on disclosure and permitted use apply. The\nauthor remains responsible for verifiable statements; generated\nsuggestions are not automatically reliable literature references.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A realistic work plan<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001515_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Zeitplan mit sechs Phasen: Frage und Zugang in Woche 1–2, Planung 3, Umsetzung 4–5, Test 6, Bericht 7 und Abschluss 8.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Zeitplan mit sechs Phasen: Frage und\nZugang in Woche 1–2, Planung 3, Umsetzung 4–5, Test 6, Bericht 7 und\nAbschluss 8.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Meilensteinplan für ein Lernprojekt.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The following freely chosen eight-week plan is an example, not a\nmandatory duration for a Bachelor’s thesis. Before registration, clarify\nexamination regulations, supervision, prerequisites, deadlines, data\nprotection and, where applicable, ethics approval. A proposal describes\nthe question, existing literature, method, required data, risks and\nexpected type of results. If access to data is at risk, the project\nneeds a viable alternative question; switching to synthetic data changes\nthe scope of the conclusions and must be explicitly agreed.<\/p>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-third\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Week<\/th>\n<th>Activity<\/th>\n<th>Evidence / decision<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1–2<\/td>\n<td>clarify question, literature and access<\/td>\n<td>proposal, review decision<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>data definition and methods plan<\/td>\n<td>data dictionary, protocol<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4–5<\/td>\n<td>implement and validate<\/td>\n<td>tests, frozen method<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>specified test and evaluation<\/td>\n<td>run log, results table<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>report and discussion<\/td>\n<td>draft, literature check<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>reproduction, corrections, presentation<\/td>\n<td>submission version and reserve<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The graphic illustrates the dependencies between phases. Data\ncollection must not start before access is clarified; the final test\nmust not begin until model selection is complete. Writing and\ndocumentation take place throughout the project. Small milestones reveal\ndelays early, particularly missing data or unclear assessment\ncriteria.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Presentation and review<\/h2>\n<p>For an example ten-minute talk, one minute is allocated to the\nproblem, two to design and data, two to methods, two to results and two\nto limitations and outlook; one minute remains in reserve. The main\nchart shows the fair comparison of both models, not an unsupported\nbenefit. Repeating the analysis program should confirm the reported\nvalues. Review also requires that the report be understandable without\nrunning the program and that the code produce the results without\nhandwritten intermediate calculations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Write a brief final conclusion. Example: on six constructed test\ndays, the predefined reservation-based forecast achieves an MAE of 7/6\nrather than 15/6 sets per day. The finding demonstrates the comparison\nmethod but allows no conclusion about a real workshop. Follow-up work\nwould require lawfully collected, temporally separated data and a\npredefined benefit threshold. This completes the teaching project, but\ndoes not constitute passing an academic Bachelor’s examination.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-12-389682\">DFG:\nGuideline 12 – Documentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – traceable documentation.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://book.the-turing-way.org/project-design/pd-overview/pd-checklist/\">The\nTuring Way: Getting Started Checklist<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – project planning,\nobjectives, methodological and organisational prerequisites.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-14-389686\">DFG:\nGuideline 14 – Authorship<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – responsibility and scholarly\ncontribution. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
